* Dogfood task 2026-07-18: write a minimal SCF-class OS-9/68000 device
* driver module (Init/Read/Write/GetStat/SetStat/Term) implementing an
* in-memory loopback FIFO, per os9-systems-dev's device-drivers.md --
* backing that skill's own flagged "Known gap": "no custom driver ...
* has actually been written and loaded to confirm the documented calling
* conventions." Full account: dogfood-report-driver-2026-07-18.md.
*
* VERIFICATION STATUS (see report for full detail):
*   - Assembles clean on r68 (0 errors), links clean on l68 (0 errors),
*     produces a genuine, CRC-valid, correctly-typed OS-9/68k module:
*     `ident` reports "68000 Dev Drv, Object Code, Sharable, System
*     State Process", Ty/La=$E01 (Drivr/Objct), Attr/Rev=$A001
*     (Sharable+SuperState/rev1) -- exactly as intended.
*   - Jump-table entries verified byte-correct by dumping the linked
*     module and cross-checking each dc.w value against the real
*     disassembled position of its routine (Init's word landed at
*     $004A, and Init's own first opcode -- `48e7 8080`, i.e.
*     `movem.l d0/a0,-(a7)` -- was confirmed sitting at that exact file
*     offset). This is genuinely `Live`-confirmed, not just "assembles."
*   - NEVER CONFIRMED, and per a from-source investigation this session
*     ran, NOT CONFIRMABLE on current os9exec: whether Init/Read/Write/
*     GetStat/SetStat/Term are ever actually invoked by the kernel.
*     os9exec's I$Attach (Source/OS9exec_core/icalls.c) is an explicit
*     dummy that always returns a fake device-table handle without
*     allocating driver static storage or calling Init; os9exec's own
*     `iniz` command doesn't exist in the C source at all; device path
*     resolution (IO_Type()/utilstuff.c) matches a fixed, hardcoded
*     13-entry enum of path types by string prefix, never by loading
*     and executing an installed driver module's own machine code. Live
*     testing (this session) confirms the practical consequence: even
*     after `load`ing this driver's paired descriptor as a resident
*     module literally named "DOGFOOD" (see dogfood-driver-loopback-desc.a),
*     `dir /DOGFOOD` and a real test program's `I$Open("/DOGFOOD")` both
*     still fail with "Module Not Found" (E$MNF), and `iniz DOGFOOD`
*     silently no-ops (no error, no device-table entry -- `devs` stays
*     empty throughout). Init's known-byte-pattern write (V_MAGIC =
*     'DOGF', below) was written specifically to let a debugger/dump
*     session prove whether Init ran; it was never reachable to test,
*     because no static storage for this driver is ever allocated by
*     os9exec in the first place.
*   - Register conventions below (a2=static storage; d1.b for
*     Read/Write's single-byte transfer) are this file's own
*     best-effort GUESS, following the one convention actually
*     documented anywhere in the skill (RBF's (a1)/(a2)/(a4)/(a5)/(a6)
*     pattern) -- device-drivers.md explicitly says SCF's own register
*     convention for these entries is undocumented. Never live-checked
*     against a real kernel call, because no real kernel call to these
*     entry points was reachable this session.
* ============================================================

 nam dogloop
 ttl DOGFOOD in-memory loopback SCF-class device driver

* ============================================================
* Module-type/attribute constants, hand-defined (not `use`d from
* /h0/DEFS/oskdefs.d -- earlier dogfood sessions found no I$/F$
* numeric-code header on this SDK disk either; hand-defining is
* the established working pattern, confirmed against
* module-format.md's documented type-code table).
* ============================================================
Drivr    set $0E
Objct    set $01
ReEnt    set $80
SuperSt  set $20
Type_Lang set (Drivr<<8)+Objct
Attr_Rev  set ((ReEnt+SuperSt)<<8)+1

* ============================================================
* Static-storage offsets (from a2 at every entry point).
* SCF's own static-storage layout is NOT documented anywhere in
* the os9-systems-dev skill (device-drivers.md explicitly flags
* this as a "Known gap" -- only field NAMES V_BUSY/V_WAKE/V_PAUS/
* V_XON are mentioned in prose, no offsets). This layout is this
* driver's OWN invention, not a claimed real-OS-9 SCF layout.
* ============================================================
V_PORT   set $00   * kernel pre-loads from descriptor's M$Port (4 bytes)
V_MAGIC  set $0A   * Init writes 'DOGF' here -- live proof Init ran (4 bytes)
V_HEAD   set $04   * FIFO read index (2 bytes)
V_TAIL   set $06   * FIFO write index (2 bytes)
V_COUNT  set $08   * bytes currently buffered (2 bytes)
V_BUF    set $0E   * FIFO byte buffer
BUFSIZE  set 64
STORESZ  set V_BUF+BUFSIZE

* ============================================================
* Driver module header + 7-entry jump table (device-drivers.md's
* documented Entry Point Table -- offsets 0/2/4/6/8/10/12 words,
* INIT/READ/WRITE/GETSTAT/SETSTAT/TERM/TRAP). M$Exec (psect's
* "entry" operand) points at this table's first word.
*
* Each jump-table dc.w entry below is a BARE label reference
* (`dc.w Init`, not `dc.w Init-dogloop`). Live-verified (dumping
* the linked module and matching each word against the real
* disassembled position of the corresponding routine): the
* linker resolves a bare dc.w symbol reference to that symbol's
* true module-start-relative offset directly, regardless of
* where the psect body itself lands (which, also confirmed live,
* is NOT immediately at file offset $30 the way the addone
* worked example's single M$Exec field made it look -- this
* module's body actually starts at $3C; the assembler
* unconditionally reserves 12 bytes, matching mod_exec's
* M$Exec/M$Excpt/M$Data slots, before the psect body regardless
* of module type). An earlier version of this file used manual
* "label-label+$30" arithmetic guessing at that $30 offset; it
* was wrong by exactly the 12-byte gap. Bare references sidestep
* the whole guess and are provably correct instead.
* ============================================================
 psect dogloop,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,1,0,dogloop

dogloop:
 dc.w Init
 dc.w Read
 dc.w Write
 dc.w GetStat
 dc.w SetStat
 dc.w Term
 dc.w 0                 * TRAP entry -- unused per skill

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Init -- kernel gives static storage zeroed except V_PORT.
* Register convention here is UNATTESTED anywhere in the skill
* (device-drivers.md's own "Known gap" note); (a2)=static storage
* is the one constant every documented driver/file-manager entry
* point shares, so that is the only register this code trusts.
* Writes a known byte pattern ('DOGF') into V_MAGIC so a live
* debugger/dump session can tell whether Init actually ran --
* never reachable to test this session, see the top-of-file
* verification status note.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Init:
 movem.l d0/a0,-(a7)
 clr.w   V_HEAD(a2)
 clr.w   V_TAIL(a2)
 clr.w   V_COUNT(a2)
 move.l  #$444F4746,V_MAGIC(a2)   * 'DOGF'
 movem.l (a7)+,d0/a0
 andi    #$FFFE,ccr               * clear carry: success
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Read -- pop one byte from the FIFO into d1.b (best-effort
* guess; no documented SCF register convention exists to copy).
* Empty FIFO -> carry set, d1.w = E$NotRdy ($0217, from
* os9-dev's error-codes.md).
* ------------------------------------------------------------
E_NOTRDY set $0217

Read:
 tst.w   V_COUNT(a2)
 beq.s   Read_Empty
 movem.l d0/a0,-(a7)
 move.w  V_HEAD(a2),d0
 lea     V_BUF(a2),a0
 move.b  0(a0,d0.w),d1
 addq.w  #1,d0
 cmpi.w  #BUFSIZE,d0
 blt.s   Read_NoWrap
 clr.w   d0
Read_NoWrap:
 move.w  d0,V_HEAD(a2)
 subq.w  #1,V_COUNT(a2)
 movem.l (a7)+,d0/a0
 andi    #$FFFE,ccr
 rts
Read_Empty:
 move.w  #E_NOTRDY,d1
 ori     #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Write -- push one byte (from d1.b, same unverified guess as
* Read) onto the FIFO. Full FIFO -> carry set, E$NotRdy.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Write:
 move.w  V_COUNT(a2),d0
 cmpi.w  #BUFSIZE,d0
 bge.s   Write_Full
 movem.l d0/a0,-(a7)
 move.w  V_TAIL(a2),d0
 lea     V_BUF(a2),a0
 move.b  d1,0(a0,d0.w)
 addq.w  #1,d0
 cmpi.w  #BUFSIZE,d0
 blt.s   Write_NoWrap
 clr.w   d0
Write_NoWrap:
 move.w  d0,V_TAIL(a2)
 addq.w  #1,V_COUNT(a2)
 movem.l (a7)+,d0/a0
 andi    #$FFFE,ccr
 rts
Write_Full:
 move.w  #E_NOTRDY,d1
 ori     #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* GetStat / SetStat -- no codes implemented; every request comes
* back E$UnkSvc ($0216) so an unrecognized status code is
* reported cleanly instead of silently doing nothing.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
E_UNKSVC set $0216

GetStat:
 move.w  #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori     #1,ccr
 rts

SetStat:
 move.w  #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori     #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Term -- per device-drivers.md, called even if Init failed
* partway; nothing dynamically allocated here, so there is
* nothing for Term to release. Clears V_MAGIC so a live dump
* can distinguish "never inited" / "inited" / "reused after
* term" states.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Term:
 clr.l   V_MAGIC(a2)
 andi    #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

 ends
